", "link": "https://seyboldjewelry.com/malca-amit/", "title": {"rendered": "MALCA-AMIT &#8211; 529"}, "content": {"rendered": "", "protected": false}}, {"id": 3123, "slug": "uber", "link": "https://seyboldjewelry.com/uber/", "title": {"rendered": "Via Uber"}, "content": {"rendered": "", "protected": false}}, {"id": 3109, "slug": "mjm-global-insurance-brokerage-group", "link": "https://seyboldjewelry.com/mjm-global-insurance-brokerage-group/", "title": {"rendered": "MJM GLOBAL INSURANCE BROKERAGE GROUP &#8211; 300"}, "content": {"rendered": "", "protected": false}}, {"id": 2958, "slug": "over-the-years", "link": "https://seyboldjewelry.com/over-the-years/", "title": {"rendered": "Over The Years"}, "content": {"rendered": "<p>Did John Seybold set out to be a jeweler? Of course not.</p>\n<p>But back at the dawn of the 20-th century when Miami was still largely terra incognito, John Seybold was a man with a vision.</p>\n<p>Armed with bread and confectionaries, he established his first business in Miami Avenue purveying baked goods to the neighborhood. Business was prolific, until a major fire burned down decades-worth of work and, soon, the baker would have to work his way up again, to save and build his business back from the ashes.</p>\n<p>The new premises were based in the buoyant Flagler road, where business was abuzz and the heartbeat of Miami was beating loud. Within a few years, John Seybold started expanding its business, buying and extending to nearby constructions.</p>\n<p>However, with the rapid development of the city, it soon became clear that the only way was up. The first three levels were constructed in 1921 by the nation renowned&nbsp;Kiehnel and Elliott architects. With a vast array of buildings registered under national heritage scheme, John Seybold was sure to work with the best in class to deliver his vision.&nbsp;This is how the Seybold Arcade was born; a landmark place filled with the latest fashions, offices, the best doctors in town and confectionaries of course!</p>\n<p>Within a few years, sever more stories were added to the main Arcade. The Seybold Building was now a reality.</p>\n<p>But it wasn&#8217;t always a jewelry building. In the 1960s, the building\u2019s first jeweler moved in. Jeffrey Buchwald, a Hungarian immigrant who first opened his jewelry shop on the boardwalk in Atlantic City, moved to Miami to escape the cold. Then the Cuban Revolution brought a wave of jewelry artisans to the Seybold, further establishing its footprint as a prime jewelry destination.</p>\n<p>Today, still located in the heart of downtown Miami, this 150,000 SF building is the third largest diamond and jewelry center in the United States.&nbsp; A nationally registered historic landmark, the Seybold building has a three-floor retail arcade with wholesale and manufacturing centers above, and houses over 300 jewelers.</p>\n<p>The world renowned Seybold\u00ae Building is recognized as a leader in the jewelry industry.</p>\n<div style=\"font-size: 12px;\">\n<p><em>Sources:</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fusco Corporation</li>\n<li>Wikipedia</li>\n<li>The New Tropic</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>\n\t\t\t<!-- START Over the years REVOLUTION SLIDER 6.6.20 --><p class=\"rs-p-wp-fix\"></p>\n\t\t\t<rs-module-wrap id=\"rev_slider_3_2_wrapper\" data-source=\"gallery\" style=\"visibility:hidden;background:#111111;padding:0;margin:0px auto;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;\">\n\t\t\t\t<rs-module id=\"rev_slider_3_2\" style=\"\" data-version=\"6.6.20\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<rs-slides style=\"overflow: hidden; position: absolute;\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<rs-slide style=\"position: absolute;\" data-key=\"rs-11\" data-title=\"Slide\" data-thumb=\"//seyboldjewelry.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/seybold-building-historic-100x50.jpg\" data-anim=\"adpr:false;\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<img decoding=\"async\" src=\"//seyboldjewelry.com/wp-content/plugins/revslider/public/assets/assets/dummy.png\" alt=\"\" title=\"Over The Years\" class=\"rev-slidebg tp-rs-img rs-lazyload\" data-lazyload=\